    part admittedGapsGuardsImplementGateR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "6bf68b2c-bbbc-4839-abbb-845124791c3e"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "1d84acd"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-21"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeFable5"; }
    verification admittedGapsGuardsReviewGate : Test { :>> id = "44a0c35e-9dc3-4528-b838-281478c22654"; :>> title = "Sprint 395 review gate — three passes per D0172"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-21"; :>> createdBy = "claudeFable5"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::inspect; :>> procedureText = "PASS 1, CLAIMS: every count above is tool output from this session; the v0.2.0 skew is git-verified ancestry, not inference. PASS 2, BOUNDARY: the deactivatability boundary is the deliberate design - both guards are unit-owned so deactivating the process removes its guard, which is CORRECT for process-specific claims and is exactly why the actors guard was NOT moved into the enrollment unit (identity integrity must survive process deactivation). PASS 3, OWN DIFF: the guards395 fix script carried three self-escaping bugs (literal backslash-n where newlines were meant) caught by re-reading before running - the authoring-mangling family again, this time caught pre-execution; and GUARD_NAMES' array length was machine-caught by the compiler. WHAT A REVIEWER SHOULD ATTACK: the standing v0.2.0 warning is a permanent WARN until someone reconciles it - if it normalizes warning-blindness, the right move is the owner-side supersede, not a guard exemption; and release-recorded matches tags to Release items by title substring, so a title naming two tags would satisfy both - acceptable while titles are one-version by convention, named here so the assumption is on record."; }
